#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
build_snapshot.py — compute one wide row per symbol, once, into DuckDB.

    sudo -u breakout /opt/breakoutscanner/.venv/bin/python \
         /opt/breakoutscanner/build_snapshot.py --universe "NIFTY Smallcap 250"
    ... --universe all          # every cached index segment
    ... --limit 40              # quick smoke test

This is the piece that makes ChartsMaze-style screening possible on your
existing box. Instead of loading 250 symbols' bars every time somebody runs a
scan, the work happens ONCE and every scanner afterwards is a WHERE clause
against `indicator_snapshot` — milliseconds instead of minutes.

WHY DUCKDB
----------
Not for raw speed — 2,000 rows x 50 columns is trivial for anything. It earns
its place because the interesting screening questions are WINDOW questions,
and those belong in SQL rather than pandas:

    "% of each sector trading above RS 80"     <- the metric ChartsMaze ranks
    "percentile rank of this stock's return    <- RS Rating, universe-relative
     across the whole universe"

In SQLite those become a pandas round trip: read everything out, group, rank,
write back. In DuckDB they are one statement, computed where the data already
is. `sector_breadth_rs80` below is the concrete example.

It is also embedded — a file, no server, no daemon — so nothing new is left
running on a 512MB box beside a live trading app.

    pip install duckdb        (one ~20MB wheel, no compilation)

MEMORY
------
Bars are loaded per symbol and released immediately — never all at once. Peak
usage is one symbol's history plus the accumulating result rows, which is a
few MB. This matters: an earlier version of the scanner segfaulted on this
box, and the cause was library incompatibility rather than memory, but there
is no reason to go looking for the real thing.

Reuses screen.py and patterns.py exactly as they are. Nothing in breakout.py
or scanner.py is touched, so detection and the existing pages behave
identically whether this has been run or not.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

